## Round-1 findings — verdicts

**P1-1 — CONFIRMED FIXED** (`:str` surrogate guard, both entry points). `encode-operand`'s `:str` case is now `(if (nil? x) (framed :string "") (encode-scalar x))` (`debruijn_code.cljc:503`), routing non-nil `:str` through the same `well-formed-utf16?` guard `:const` strings get. `image-defect` also rejects structurally: `operand-kind-checks` `:str` is now `(fn [x] (or (nil? x) (and (string? x) (well-formed-utf16? x))))` (`:650`). Hand-trace of `[[:gensym "\uD800"] [:return]]`: `encode-scalar` → `scalar-class` → `(string? v) (when (well-formed-utf16? v) :string)` → nil (lone high surrogate at a terminal code unit is `false` in `well-formed-utf16?`, `:263-266`) → falls to the qualified `(throw (ex-info … {:rule :unsupported-value …}))` (`:460-461`). Structural path returns `{:rule :operand-kind, :pc 0}`. Both correct.

**P1-2 — CONFIRMED FIXED** (and generalizes). `walk-body` now returns `{:conflict pc}` when a pc it reaches is already owned under a different chain (`:763-766`), and `all-body-chains` returns `{:conflict entry}` when a queued body entry disagrees with an owned chain (`:796-798`). Hand-traced the reviewer's fixture in **both** orders — `[[:closure 2 3] [:closure 1 3] …]` and swapped — both yield `{:rule :scope-conflict, :pc 3}` (the second declared chain `'(1)` meets owned `'(2)` at the queue level). Then constructed the three adversarial variants the prompt asks about, all caught by the *same* `(not= chain-a chain-b)` comparison:
- **three declarations** `[[:closure 1 3] [:closure 2 3] [:closure 3 3] …]` → second differing body triggers conflict;
- **cross-body `:jump`** `[[:closure 1 4] [:jump 4] [:return] [:return] [:return]]` → root body (chain `'()`) reaches pc 4 via the jump, closure body declares pc 4 with chain `'(1)` → conflict at 4;
- **length-differing chain** — `not=` compares whole vectors, so any length or content difference trips it.

Re-joins *within* one body (a `:branch-false` loop reaching an already-owned pc with the *same* chain) are correctly skipped (`:764`), so no false positive and no BFS non-termination. `scope-defect` was updated to the new `{:owner …}`/`{:conflict …}` return shape (`:830-832`); no other caller of `all-body-chains`/`walk-body` exists.

**P2-1 — CONFIRMED FIXED** (JVM only; CLJS/CLJD untouched). `host-double?` is now `#?(:clj (instance? Double v) :default (and (number? v) (not (host-long? v)) (not (host-ratio? v)) (not (host-bigint? v))))` (`:342-343`). On the JVM, BigDecimal/Float are no longer `Double` → `scalar-class` nil → qualified `:unsupported-value`; CLJS and CLJD both take `:default` (no `:clj` match), which is the unchanged complement rule. The report's own caveat stands: P2-1 has **no dedicated pinned-literal test** (verified only manually) — see new-finding note below.

**P2-2 — CONFIRM FIXED**, but the CLJD branch has a defect — see **NEW P2** below. The cross-host test `unsupported-value-is-refused-not-silently-hashed` (`debruijn_code_test.cljc:202-216`) exercises all four sites: `encode-scalar` direct, `image-hash` of a `:const`, `image-defect` of a `:str` (returns `:operand-kind`, not a throw — so "four throw sites" is loose wording for "three throws + one structural rejection", but all four *entry points* are covered), and `image-hash` of a `:str`. The `unsupported-value-rule` helper's catch clause `#?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs :default)` (`:199`) is the correct per-host catch-all spelling.

**P3-1 — CONFIRMED FIXED.** `saturated-operands` now `#{[:gensym 1] [:stream-make 1] [:call 2] [:ffi-call 2]}` (`:679`).

**P3-2 — CONFIRMED FIXED.** The JVM `:ratio` branch range-checks both components against `Long/MIN_VALUE`/`MAX_VALUE` and throws the qualified diagnostic (`:436-442`); the `:cljs`/`:cljd` stubs are unchanged dead code.

**P3-3 — CONFIRMED FIXED.** `nonneg-int?` gained `#?(:cljs (js/Number.isSafeInteger x) :default true)` (`:636`), and `target-bounds-rule` switched its `:pc` check from `(integer? x)` to `(nonneg-int? x)` (`:701`) so `:pc` is bounded too. CLJS `[:call 1e30 true]` now yields `:operand-kind`; JVM was already correct (`integer?` false for a Double).

**P3-4 — CONFIRMED FIXED, minimal blast radius.** The descriptor (`:113-141`) is now pure data — `:image-hash "SHA-256(…)"` removed, `:dim/lift-to` collapsed to `[:yin.code/*]`. The new test asserts no string anywhere: `(not (some #(some string? (tree-seq coll? seq %)) dc/descriptor))` (`:353-354`) — I verified by inspection that the descriptor contains only keywords, numbers, booleans, and nested vectors/maps. `encode-image` never touches the descriptor, so instruction bytes are provably unchanged; only `descriptor-hash` (hence `image-hash`) forked, and the re-pin is documented (`:289-295`).

**P3-5 — CONFIRMED FIXED.** `image-scalar-classes` now `keep-indexed` over `:data`-kind operands across every tuple (`:553-567`), strictly more conservative than the `:const`-only floor the design wording describes — no weakening.

