## NEW findings

**NEW P2 — CLJD `lone-surrogate` uses static-method slash syntax for a named constructor.** `test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:192`: `:cljd (String/fromCharCode 0xD800)`. Dart's `String.fromCharCode` is a **factory/named constructor**, not a static method, and this codebase's ClojureDart convention for named constructors is **dot** syntax — `String.fromCharCodes` (`test/dao/jing/cbor_fixtures.cljc:164`), `dart:core/String.fromCharCodes` (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:192`), `BigInt.parse` (`test/dao/jing/cbor_test.cljc:345`). Slash (`/`) is used only for genuine static methods on the lowercase core types (`double/parse`, `int/parse` in `src/cljc/yang/python.cljc:110,113`). `String/fromCharCode` therefore resolves `String` as a namespace and `fromCharCode` as a var in it — likely a "could not resolve" at CLJD compile time, which would break the whole test namespace on that lane (not just this deftest). This is exactly the branch the implementer flagged UNVERIFIED. Smallest fix: `:cljd (String.fromCharCode 0xD800)`, then actually run the CLJD lane (the orchestrator's "CLJD 0 failures" is not trustworthy against this fixture given the implementer reported CLJD denied).

**NEW P3 — P2-1 has no pinned-literal regression test.** `host-double?` (`:342`) was changed to `instance? Double` on JVM, but no test asserts that a JVM BigDecimal/Float is refused (the report admits this was "verified manually"). A one-line `#?(:clj (is (= :unsupported-value (unsupported-value-rule #(dc/encode-scalar 0.1M)))))` would pin it and prevent a silent regression to the old complement rule. Not blocking, but worth closing while this exact class of refusal is being pinned.

